Task 4: Configure Real Build Notifications
Jenkins supports two realistic, verifiable notification mechanisms without requiring any external paid service: (1) structured log-based notifications printed directly in the post block (already added in Task 3), and (2) real SMTP email notifications using the Email Extension plugin configured against a mail relay you control. This task configures both, using the post block already present in the Jenkinsfile above â you will not need to hand-edit the failure block again.

Subtask 4.1: Verify Log-Based Notifications (Already Working)
The post block created in Subtask 3.2 already prints clear, distinct messages for always, success, failure, and unstable outcomes. Confirm this works right now:

Open the last build's Console Output in Jenkins
Scroll to the bottom and confirm you see a line beginning with SUCCESS: Build #<n> completed all stages...
This is a fully functional notification mechanism: any external log-monitoring tool (e.g., a script tailing Jenkins logs, or a monitoring agent) can watch for the SUCCESS, FAILURE, or UNSTABLE keywords and trigger alerts
Subtask 4.2: Install and Configure the Email Extension Plugin
Go to Manage Jenkins > Manage Plugins > Available, search for Email Extension Plugin, install it, and restart Jenkins if prompted
Go to Manage Jenkins > Configure System, scroll to Extended E-mail Notification
Configure the SMTP server. For this lab, use the local Postfix mail relay installed below rather than an external account:
# Install a local sendmail-compatible relay so Jenkins has a real SMTP target
sudo apt install postfix -y
# During the interactive prompt, choose "Local only" as the configuration type
sudo systemctl status postfix --no-pager
Back in Configure System > Extended E-mail Notification:
SMTP server: localhost
SMTP Port: 25
Default user e-mail suffix: @localhost
Click Advanced, leave authentication and SSL unchecked since this is a local relay
Under Default Recipients (in the same section), enter admin@localhost
Click Apply and Save
Subtask 4.3: Add the emailext Step to the Jenkinsfile
Edit the Jenkinsfile using nano (or your preferred editor) and replace the entire existing post block with the version below, which adds a working emailext call alongside the existing log messages:

cd ~/jenkins-demo-app
nano Jenkinsfile
Replace the whole post { ... } block at the end of the file with exactly this:

    post {
        always {
            echo "Pipeline execution completed for build #${BUILD_NUMBER}"
            sh 'rm -f app.log'
            cleanWs()
        }
        success {
            echo "SUCCESS: Build #${BUILD_NUMBER} completed all stages: Checkout -> Build -> Unit Tests -> Quality Check -> Package -> Integration Test -> Deploy"
            emailext (
                subject: "Jenkins Build SUCCESS: ${env.JOB_NAME} #${env.BUILD_NUMBER}",
                body: "Good news! The pipeline ${env.JOB_NAME} build #${env.BUILD_NUMBER} completed successfully.\n\nView details: ${env.BUILD_URL}",
                to: 'admin@localhost'
            );
        }
        failure {
            echo "FAILURE: Build #${BUILD_NUMBER} failed. Check the stage logs above to identify the failing stage."
            emailext (
                subject: "Jenkins Build FAILED: ${env.JOB_NAME} #${env.BUILD_NUMBER}",
                body: "The pipeline ${env.JOB_NAME} build #${env.BUILD_NUMBER} failed.\n\nCheck console output at: ${env.BUILD_URL}console",
                to: 'admin@localhost'
            );
        }
        unstable {
            echo "UNSTABLE: Build #${BUILD_NUMBER} completed with test failures."
            emailext (
                subject: "Jenkins Build UNSTABLE: ${env.JOB_NAME} #${env.BUILD_NUMBER}",
                body: "The pipeline ${env.JOB_NAME} build #${env.BUILD_NUMBER} is unstable (tests failed).\n\nCheck test results at: ${env.BUILD_URL}testReport",
                to: 'admin@localhost'
            );
        }
    }
Save and exit the editor (in nano: Ctrl+O, Enter, Ctrl+X).

