Troubleshooting
If the pipeline fails at the Checkout stage with a "repository not found" error, what does this tell you about how Jenkins resolves the repository path configured in the job, and how would you confirm the exact path Jenkins is using versus the path on your filesystem?
If emailext reports "Sending email" in the console but no message ever appears in /var/mail/admin, what steps would you take to determine whether the problem is in Jenkins' plugin configuration, in Postfix's delivery, or in mailbox permissions â and which log file would you check first?
Conclusion
In this lab, students installed Jenkins from scratch, built a complete Maven-based Java application with unit tests and code coverage, and expressed the entire build lifecycle as a version-controlled Jenkinsfile covering checkout, build, test, quality checks, packaging, integration testing, and deployment. Students then configured genuine, verifiable notification channels â structured console logging and real SMTP email delivery through a local Postfix relay â replacing any reliance on external or unverifiable services. By deliberately introducing and then fixing a failing test, students observed how Jenkins reports failures across the Stage View, console output, and build history, and learned to use the Test Result Trend graph to distinguish regressions from recoveries. These skills form the practical foundation of continuous integration: automated, observable, and reliably notified build pipelines that catch problems immediately after they are introduced.
